//! Type alias chain and type-level resolution.
== substitute      540
//! Structural substitution for user-defined generic type aliases.
//!
//! `type Assign<T, U> = Omit<T, keyof U> & U` declares type parameters `T`/`U`
//! that are opaque names until a call site supplies concrete arguments, e.g.
//! `Assign<HTMLProps<'div'>, SelectRootBaseProps<T>>`. Previously the resolver
//! looked up `Assign` by name and resolved its body directly — `T`/`U` were
//! never replaced with the caller's arguments, so `Omit<T, keyof U>` resolved
//! against literal type names `"T"`/`"U"` (unresolvable) instead of the real
== named      675
//! Named type resolution.
== chain      741
//! Props chain resolution — the recursive core of prop resolution.
== func      167
//! Function type and typeof resolution.
== callee      391
//! The props of a component a generic call builds, read from what the callee returns.
//!
//! `const Button = factory<ButtonFactory>(render)` is whatever `factory` returns. With the callee's type parameters
//! bound to the call's type arguments, the props parameter of the React component type in its result is the
//! component's props, as TypeScript (and react-docgen-typescript) reads them: Mantine's `factory` returns
//! `MantineComponent<Payload>`, whose props are `Payload['props']` plus `component` and `renderRoot`. The extractor's
//! choice among the type arguments (`extractor/wrapper.rs`) is the fallback for a callee with no known result. Nothing
//! here names a library; the React component types are React's own.
== evaluate      958
//! The type-level evaluation a props base needs before its props can be listed, when the operands are known: the branch
//! a conditional takes for a literal (`T extends keyof Recipes ? … : …` with `T = "button"`), a member read through
//! an indexed access (`Recipes["button"]["__type"]`, generic interface members included), and the keys a mapped type
//! ranges over (`{ [K in keyof Variant]?: … }`). Anything that needs a checker (an unknown operand, a generic left
//! unbound, an inferred type) evaluates to `None`, and the caller keeps its existing handling.
//!
//! Its `depth` counts only its own steps: it reads declarations and never re-enters the resolver, so callers start it
//! at 0 instead of passing the resolver's depth, which a deep chain (csstype's `Fallback<…>` under Chakra's style
== extends      223
//! Extends ref resolution.
== memo      343
//! Interface chains and named types resolved once per run and shared by every component that reaches them.
//!
//! A non-generic interface's chain depends on more than the interface: on the component's cycle sets (an ancestor
//! already being resolved cuts a branch short), on the type parameters in scope, on the component's own parameter
//! defaults, and on the depth it is reached at. So a recording keeps every read the resolution made of those
//! (`StateReads`), and another component reuses it only when its own state gives the same answer to each one. Then
//! the resolution would take exactly the same steps, so the recording replays its effects instead: the chain itself,
//! with its props shared rather than copied, its diagnostics, the cycle-set additions, whether it showed
== deps      110
//! What a component's resolution depended on, so watch mode re-resolves exactly the components an edit can change. A
//! component resolves on one thread from start to finish, so the recorder is thread-local and scoped to one
//! `resolve_component` call; lookups record into it without every resolver function threading a collector through.

